    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Puts Snow Tracks on Five SUVs and Trucks, Unloads Them at SEMA 2026 as the Snowhunter Bunch\nCouple Spent $1 Million on an Endless Cruise, Were Kicked Off It After 2 Years\n19 photos\nPhoto: Randy Cassingham with permission from ResidentialCruising.com\nThere's no use in crying over spilled milk, but the urge to do so must feel irresistible when you sold your home and all your land-bound possessions in order to afford the milk that someone else spilled for you. Or, in this case, the cruise ship that should have kept circumnavigating the world non-stop ever 3.5 years.\nEarlier this month, Villa Vie Residences (VVR), a Florida-based startup that promised never-ending cruises that would change the way people lived \"globally\" at sea, announced that their flagship Odyssey would cease operations immediately . The result was 203 guests and 156 crew getting kicked off the ship and left stranded in a port in Cambodia, with the exception of a skeleton crew that would continue on for maintenance.\nAs of the Odyssey, it would remain laid up until management could find a solution out of the predicament, whether that be a new investor and cash infusion or file for bankruptcy. The guests were given less than six hours to gather all their belongings and disembark, without as much as being offered some guidance regarding local accommodation, at least at first.\nFor them, the Odyssey wasn't yet another cruise ship, small as it might be. This was their home or the place they hopped to call that for at least the next 15 years, because that's what VVR had promised them.\nPhoto: Villa Vie Residences That's what this novel way of living at sea entailed: full ownership of a cabin on a ship that would circumnavigate the globe continuously every 3.5 years, before it would head in for regular maintenance and then depart again. VVR offered at-sea residences (villas), but they also sold more traditional cruises – the segments that made up that 3.5-year continuous cruise.\nIt was imagined as an all-encompassing solution for anyone for whom a cruise is the perfect way to spend the time. You could either dip a toe and get onboard as an occasional guest, joining the residents for cruises ranging from one week to a couple of months, or you could go all-in and buy a cabin – to live in or to rent.\nBuying a cabin meant getting to enjoy a new lifestyle , one where you could combine the advantages of being a perpetual tourist/traveler to residential comfort. You could basically work from anywhere, while having all the house chores handled by someone else, with the bonus of having views that changed everyday.\nPhoto: Villa Vie Residences To boot, VVR promised all these things at prices that could still be called affordable. At the time the Odyssey was launched, VVR cited the high costs of living and the higher prices of other cruises as perfect examples of why this type of lifestyle could not fail.\nYou have to admit, the idea is appealing even today, when we know that the Odyssey pulled into port, kicked off all the guests, and was declared \"over\" despite promises that the dream would live on for at least 15 years after the 2024 departure.\nGuests still in Cambodia, still waiting for some kind of clarification from the VVR management, are starting to talk to the media, offering a glimpse into their state of mind, their exact financial predicament, and their thoughts on how the adventure has ended. Among them is Randy Cassingham, who, with his wife Kit, was one of the full-time owners on the Odyssey .\nPhoto: Villa Vie Residences Cassingham is an American writer, humorist, and a pioneer in online publishing. His This Is True website was the first for-profit email letter, and he continues to be an active and well-regarded publisher of content. He was one of the first to embrace the Odyssey dream, buying a villa and moving onboard the second the ship was cleared for departure out of Belfast in 2024.\nSome reports online claim that he was a Founder, so not a simple Resident, which would mean he'd invested in the VVR enterprise and, as such, had a stake in it. Cassingham never said so publicly, but he was always a supporter and cheerleader of the Odyssey dream.\nHe continues to be even now, when VVR has kicked him and his wife off the ship with everyone else. Speaking to the media out of Cambodia, he says they spent close to $1 million for what they believed would be their long-term, if not forever, home.\nPhoto: Villa Vie Residences This amount includes the $370,000 to $380,000 they initially paid for their cabin on the Odyssey and the $7,000 a month in expenses since they boarded. Every villa on the Odyssey came with a fixed price tag depending on its location and amenities (balcony, size, the usual on a cruise ship), but residents still had to pay variable amounts on a monthly basis for stuff like housekeeping, medical visits, food and drinks, and other options.\nCassingham says this two-year experience was \"great, money aside,\" adding that it was \"fantastic\" that they could travel the world and have so many experience during this time. Neither he nor his wife has any regrets, even though they sold their home in the U.S. and even their car in order to get onboard.\nIt seems like the key to this kind of upbeat attitude in the face of financial disaster comes from the deliberate decision to get into the adventure knowing that it might fail at any moment. Cassingham has previously spoken about a \"cascade of problems\" that he and all residents had witnessed in 2026, including meeting with management to try and raise money from the residents to pay for fuel and cost-cutting measures like slower travels and longer stays in port.\nPhoto: Villa Vie Residences Cassignham doesn't expect any kind of refund from VVR, because he knows they've run out of money. He also knows that he doesn't really \"own\" anything on the Odyssey : despite the pompous-sounding terminology, VVR sold rights to occupy, not actual ownership.\nStill, he insists, the past two years have been a dream of an adventure, and they were worth it.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVilla Vie Residences Odyssey cruise ship Cruise liner watercraft controversy\nAbout the author: Elena Gorgan\nElena has been writing for a living since 2006 and, as a journalist, she has put her double major in English and Spanish to good use. She covers automotive and mobility topics like cars and bicycles, and she always knows the shows worth watching on Netflix and friends. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2012 Chevy Camaro ZL1 With One Owner in the Papers for Sale, Muscle Car Looks Great",
